    pinboard players groups ["Wanted change","Wanted peace","Trusted no one"] cards [{"id":"hitler","group":1,"heading":"Hitler","lines":["Germany"],"token":"hitler_t"},{"id":"mussolini","group":1,"heading":"Mussolini","lines":["Italy"],"token":"mussolini_t"},{"id":"chamberlain_c","group":2,"heading":"Chamberlain","lines":["Britain"],"token":"chamberlain_t"},{"id":"daladier","group":2,"heading":"Daladier","lines":["France"],"token":"daladier_t"},{"id":"stalin_c","group":3,"heading":"Stalin","lines":["Soviet Union"],"token":"stalin_t","stamp":"PACT"}] hidden true
    portrait hitler_t of "Adolf Hitler" token true face [0.33,0.08,0.32,0.24] hidden true
    portrait mussolini_t of "Benito Mussolini" token true face [0.3,0.06,0.36,0.25] hidden true
    portrait chamberlain_t of "Neville Chamberlain" token true face [0.24,0.06,0.56,0.44] hidden true
    portrait daladier_t of "Édouard Daladier" token true face [0.17,0.1,0.56,0.46] hidden true
    portrait stalin_t of "Joseph Stalin" token true face [0.25,0.22,0.46,0.36] hidden true

A: On the first of September 1939, German troops crossed into Poland, and barriers like this one came down.
    draw barrier

    hide hitler_t_name mussolini_t_name chamberlain_t_name daladier_t_name stalin_t_name

B: Twenty years earlier, a treaty had ended the Great War. So how did Europe get from that peace to this barrier?

    erase barrier transition cut

A: Historians answer in layers: long causes that made a war possible, short ones that made it come, and a trigger that set the day.
    draw munich_bg causes_label_1 causes_rule_1 causes_label_2 causes_rule_2 causes_label_3 causes_rule_3

A: The long causes begin with the peace itself. This is the Hall of Mirrors at Versailles in June 1919, where the Germans came to sign a treaty written without them.
    draw versailles

    erase versailles transition cut

B: Its clauses read like a charge sheet, and the first of them stung most: Article 231 put the blame for the whole war on Germany and her allies.
    draw treaty_c

A: Here is the treaty as London printed it, signatures in facsimile; Germans called it a dictated peace, a [de:Diktat].
    draw treaty transition.from treaty_c transition.match card

B: From that guilt came reparations: in 1921 the bill was set at a hundred and thirty-two billion gold marks.

A: Germany lost a tenth of its people, and union with Austria was forbidden.

    erase treaty

B: Its army was cut to a hundred thousand men, and the Rhineland, on France's border, was to hold no troops at all; so nearly every party in Germany promised to undo the treaty.
    turn "treaty_c"

A: The peace did have a guardian, the League of Nations, meeting here in Geneva; but it had no army of its own, and America never joined.
    draw geneva

B: That weakness showed when Italy invaded Ethiopia: its emperor, Haile Selassie, came to Geneva to plead for help, and got sanctions that did not stop the war.
    draw selassie

    erase selassie transition cut

A: So two long causes: a peace Germany hated, and no one ready to enforce it.
    draw league_c

B: Then a third blow came from across the Atlantic. In October 1929 the market crashed on Wall Street, crowds gathered outside the exchange, and American banks called home their loans to Germany.
    draw wallstreet

    hide munich_bg causes_label_1 causes_rule_1 causes_label_2 causes_rule_2 causes_label_3 causes_rule_3 treaty_c league_c

    erase wallstreet transition cut

A: Without those loans German firms failed, and by early 1932 nearly six million Germans were out of work, one figure here for each million.
    population jobless fit.h 120 fit.w 400 fit.x 300 fit.y 520 colors.jobless #b5482e count 6 states.jobless 6 order rows

B: And a party that had won under three per cent of the vote in 1928 was rising.
    scale vote label "Nazi vote" unit % min 0 max 50 value 37.3 markers [{"value":2.6,"label":"1928"}]

    ask "What share of the vote did they win in July 1932? Click the line." on vote store g default "20"
        right: Close: you said {g}; it was {g.true}, and they were the largest party in the Reichstag.
        wrong: You said {g}; it was {g.true}, enough to make them the largest party in the Reichstag.

A: Six months later, on the thirtieth of January 1933, Hitler stands among his party's leaders: that day, the President made him chancellor.
    draw chancellor

    hide vote jobless

    show munich_bg causes_label_1 causes_rule_1 causes_label_2 causes_rule_2 causes_label_3 causes_rule_3 treaty_c league_c

    erase chancellor transition cut

B: That is the third long cause: a slump that gave power to the one man who meant to use the other two.
    draw slump_c

A: Those three made a war possible. What made it come were gambles: between 1936 and 1939, Hitler bet four times that no one would stop him.
    focus treaty_c league_c slump_c

    hide causes_label_1 causes_rule_1 causes_label_2 causes_rule_2 causes_label_3 causes_rule_3 treaty_c league_c slump_c

B: Four of the players stand behind these cards, at Munich in 1938. Hitler wants the treaty torn up and land in the east, Mussolini an empire; Chamberlain and Daladier want no second war.
    draw hitler mussolini chamberlain_c daladier

A: The fifth, Stalin, was not in the room, and he trusted none of them.
    draw stalin_c

    hide munich_bg causes players

A: On a map of central Europe, with today's borders, every gamble starts in Berlin.
    draw country_germany country_poland country_czechia country_austria country_slovakia country_hungary country_switzerland country_belgium country_netherlands country_luxembourg marker_0 marker_label_0 parallel true

B: The first came in March 1936, when German troops marched into the Rhineland, against the treaty, and France and Britain did nothing.
    draw marker_1 marker_label_1

A: Two years later, in March 1938, they crossed into Austria, and were cheered in.
    draw marker_2 marker_label_2

B: In Vienna, crowds packed the Heldenplatz to hear Hitler proclaim the union; the treaty had forbidden it, and again no one moved.
    draw heldenplatz transition.from marker_2 transition.match place transition.to crowd

    erase heldenplatz transition cut

A: That autumn he demanded the Sudetenland, the German-speaking rim of Czechoslovakia, and threatened war.
    draw marker_3 marker_label_3

B: Chamberlain flew home from Munich, where Britain and France gave Hitler the Sudetenland, waving a paper Hitler had also signed; that evening he called it peace for our time.
    draw heston

A: A Dutch newsreel that week shows the papers going out; one front page reads: it is peace.
    draw newsreel

    erase newsreel transition cut

B: Six months later he took Prague, land that was not German at all, and Britain and France at last promised to defend Poland.
    draw marker_4 marker_label_4

    hide country_germany country_poland country_czechia country_austria country_slovakia country_hungary country_switzerland country_belgium country_netherlands country_luxembourg marker_0 marker_label_0 marker_1 marker_label_1 marker_2 marker_label_2 marker_3 marker_label_3 marker_4 marker_label_4

    show munich_bg causes_label_1 causes_rule_1 causes_label_2 causes_rule_2 causes_label_3 causes_rule_3 treaty_c league_c slump_c

A: Back on the board, those four gambles are the short causes, and each one taught Hitler something. The Rhineland taught him that France would not march alone.
    draw rhine_c

B: Austria taught him that a forbidden union could be cheered in.
    draw austria_c

A: Munich, that Britain and France would sign away another country's border to keep the peace.
    draw munich_c munich_c_stamp

B: But Prague taught the others at last that his word was worth nothing, so when he turned to Poland they had promised to fight.
    draw prague_c

A: That left the trigger, and in August 1939 it came as a handshake that nobody had expected.

    hide causes_label_1 causes_rule_1 causes_label_2 causes_rule_2 causes_label_3 causes_rule_3 treaty_c league_c slump_c rhine_c austria_c munich_c prague_c munich_c_stamp

    show hitler mussolini chamberlain_c daladier stalin_c

B: In the Kremlin, on the twenty-third of August, Stalin shook hands with Hitler's foreign minister, Ribbentrop, on a pact that neither would attack the other.
    draw pact

    erase pact transition cut

    draw stalin_c_stamp

A: So the man who trusted no one now stood beside Hitler.
    focus hitler stalin_c

    hide players

    show causes_label_1 causes_rule_1 causes_label_2 causes_rule_2 causes_label_3 causes_rule_3 treaty_c league_c slump_c rhine_c austria_c munich_c prague_c munich_c_stamp

B: And a second paper went with the pact, one that Moscow denied existed until 1989.
    draw protocol_c

A: This is that secret protocol. Its second point drew a line through Poland along three rivers, the Narew, the Vistula and the San: the west for Hitler, the east for Stalin.
    draw protocol transition.from protocol_c transition.match card

B: With Russia out of the way, Hitler invaded Poland on the first of September; two days later Britain and France, bound by their promise, declared war.
    erase protocol

A: So, from that peace to that barrier: the treaty left Germany angry, the League left it unchecked, and the slump put Hitler in charge.
    focus treaty_c league_c slump_c

B: But the war came because each gamble that went unanswered made the next one look safe,
    focus rhine_c austria_c munich_c prague_c

B: until only war was left to answer it, at a barrier like this one.
    draw barrier transition dip

    hide munich_bg causes causes_label_1 causes_rule_1 causes_label_2 causes_rule_2 causes_label_3 causes_rule_3 treaty_c league_c slump_c rhine_c austria_c munich_c prague_c munich_c_stamp protocol_c

    questions true

    quiz "Which clause put the blame for the war on Germany?"
        right: Article 231, the war-guilt clause; the reparations followed from it.
        wrong: Think of the clause the Germans hated most, the one about guilt.
        + Article 231
        * Article 80
        * Article 160

    quiz "Why could the League not stop Italy in Ethiopia?"
        right: It had no army of its own, and its sanctions did not stop the war.
        wrong: Haile Selassie did come to Geneva; what did the League lack?
        + No army, and weak sanctions
        * Italy was not a member
        * Ethiopia never asked

    quiz "What did France and Britain do when German troops entered the Rhineland in 1936?"
        right: They protested and did nothing, and the next gamble looked safer.
        wrong: Remember: not one of the four gambles was answered with force.
        + Protested, and did nothing
        * Sent in troops
        * Declared war

    quiz "Why did the pact with Stalin matter for the attack on Poland?"
        right: With Russia out of the way, and Poland shared in secret, Hitler could attack.
        wrong: Ask what Hitler feared in the east before August 1939.
        + No war with Russia to fear
        * Stalin would fight Britain
        * It gave Austria back

    quiz "What did the secret protocol of August 1939 do?"
        right: It drew a line through Poland: the west for Hitler, the east for Stalin.
        wrong: Think of the line drawn along three rivers.
        + Split Poland between them
        * Gave Austria to Germany
        * Promised to defend Poland
---
