    '|a||Without those loans German firms failed, and by early 1932 nearly six million Germans were out of work, one figure here for each million.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|b||And a party that had won under three per cent of the vote in 1928 was rising.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||What share of the vote did they win in July 1932? Click the line.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||Six months later, on the thirtieth of January 1933, Hitler stands among his party''s leaders: that day, the President made him chancellor.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|b||That is the third long cause: a slump that gave power to the one man who meant to use the other two.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||Those three made a war possible. What made it come were gambles: between 1936 and 1939, Hitler bet four times that no one would stop him.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|b||Four of the players stand behind these cards, at Munich in 1938. Hitler wants the treaty torn up and land in the east, Mussolini an empire; Chamberlain and Daladier want no second war.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||The fifth, Stalin, was not in the room, and he trusted none of them.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||On a map of central Europe, with today''s borders, every gamble starts in Berlin.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|b||The first came in March 1936, when German troops marched into the Rhineland, against the treaty, and France and Britain did nothing.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||Two years later, in March 1938, they crossed into Austria, and were cheered in.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|b||In Vienna, crowds packed the Heldenplatz to hear Hitler proclaim the union; the treaty had forbidden it, and again no one moved.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||That autumn he demanded the Sudetenland, the German-speaking rim of Czechoslovakia, and threatened war.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|b||Chamberlain flew home from Munich, where Britain and France gave Hitler the Sudetenland, waving a paper Hitler had also signed; that evening he called it peace for our time.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||A Dutch newsreel that week shows the papers going out; one front page reads: it is peace.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|b||Six months later he took Prague, land that was not German at all, and Britain and France at last promised to defend Poland.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||Back on the board, those four gambles are the short causes, and each one taught Hitler something. The Rhineland taught him that France would not march alone.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|b||Austria taught him that a forbidden union could be cheered in.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||Munich, that Britain and France would sign away another country''s border to keep the peace.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|b||But Prague taught the others at last that his word was worth nothing, so when he turned to Poland they had promised to fight.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||That left the trigger, and in August 1939 it came as a handshake that nobody had expected.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|b||In the Kremlin, on the twenty-third of August, Stalin shook hands with Hitler''s foreign minister, Ribbentrop, on a pact that neither would attack the other.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||So the man who trusted no one now stood beside Hitler.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|b||And a second paper went with the pact, one that Moscow denied existed until 1989.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||This is that secret protocol. Its second point drew a line through Poland along three rivers, the Narew, the Vistula and the San: the west for Hitler, the east for Stalin.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|b||With Russia out of the way, Hitler invaded Poland on the first of September; two days later Britain and France, bound by their promise, declared war.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||So, from that peace to that barrier: the treaty left Germany angry, the League left it unchecked, and the slump put Hitler in charge.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|b||But the war came because each gamble that went unanswered made the next one look safe,':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|b||until only war was left to answer it, at a barrier like this one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||Which clause put the blame for the war on Germany?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||Article 231, the war-guilt clause; the reparations followed from it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||Why could the League not stop Italy in Ethiopia?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||It had no army of its own, and its sanctions did not stop the war.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||What did France and Britain do when German troops entered the Rhineland in 1936?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||They protested and did nothing, and the next gamble looked safer.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||Why did the pact with Stalin matter for the attack on Poland?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||With Russia out of the way, and Poland shared in secret, Hitler could attack.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||What did the secret protocol of August 1939 do?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||It drew a line through Poland: the west for Hitler, the east for Stalin.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||Think of the clause the Germans hated most, the one about guilt.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||Haile Selassie did come to Geneva; what did the League lack?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||Remember: not one of the four gambles was answered with force.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||Ask what Hitler feared in the east before August 1939.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||Think of the line drawn along three rivers.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||Yes.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||Right.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
